  <h1>Boiler Leak: How to Tell If It's the Boiler, a Pipe, or a Radiator Before We Arrive</h1>
  <p>"My boiler's leaking" is one of the most common things we hear on the phone, and more often than you'd think, the boiler itself is fine. The water's coming from somewhere else in the system — a pipe, a radiator, sometimes even a different appliance entirely. It's a different kind of call to a full boiler breakdown in York, where nothing's working at all, but it still catches people out because a leak can look far more serious than it actually is. Working out roughly where it's coming from before you call saves you some worry and means we turn up already knowing what we're likely dealing with, rather than starting from zero on your doorstep.</p>

  <h2>Find the actual source, not just the damp patch</h2>
  <p>A stain on the ceiling or a damp patch on the carpet tells you water's been somewhere, not where it's coming from right now. Trace it back as far as you can, following the damp upward or along a pipe run until you find where the water's actually appearing, even if that means getting a torch out and having a look behind furniture or under a cupboard. Dry the area with a cloth or kitchen roll, then check back after half an hour or so to see where the new water shows up first.</p>

  <h2>Signs it's the boiler itself</h2>
  <p>A boiler leak usually shows up at the base or front of the unit, sometimes as a slow drip you can hear before you see it. It can come from a worn internal seal, the pump, or a joint inside the casing that's loosened with age. If the pressure gauge on the front is also behaving oddly, dropping steadily or needing topping up every few days, that's another strong clue it's the boiler losing water internally rather than anything further down the system.</p>

  <h2>Signs it's a pipe, not the boiler</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A white plastic pipe dripping outside the house</strong> — that's the pressure relief valve doing its job. It points to the system pressure being too high, not necessarily a fault with the pipe itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Damp or staining some distance from the boiler</strong> — a corroded joint or a pinhole in old copper pipework can show up well away from where it's actually leaking, especially if the pipe runs behind a wall or under a floor.</li>
    <li><strong>A new patch of damp after cold weather</strong> — pipework in an unheated loft or garage can crack if it's frozen and then thaws, which is more common than people expect in an older York house.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Signs it's a radiator, not the boiler at all</h2>
  <p>If the water's pooling directly underneath one particular radiator and nowhere else, it's rarely the boiler. Check both ends: the valve you'd normally turn by hand, and the lockshield valve at the other end, which usually needs a spanner or pliers to adjust. A slightly loose valve or a worn seal on one of these is a much smaller job than a boiler leak, and it's one of the more common things we find once we've actually had a look.</p>

  <h2>What's safe to check yourself</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the pressure gauge and note whether it's climbing, dropping, or steady</li>
    <li>Trace the water back to its actual source rather than just the stain it's left behind</li>
    <li>Dry the area and check again after half an hour to see where it reappears first</li>
    <li>Have a look at the valves on any radiator near the leak for obvious dampness around the joint</li>
  </ol>
  <p>What's not worth doing is opening the boiler casing or taking anything apart inside it yourself. Whatever's going on in there sits close to gas-carrying parts, and that's always a job for a Gas Safe registered engineer, whatever the leak actually turns out to be. If the water's coming fast rather than a slow drip, our guide on <a href="/advice-centre/boiler-leak-turn-off-water/">where to shut off the water before we get there</a> covers what to do in the meantime.</p>

  <h2>Getting it sorted</h2>
